nthe actions below are proposed future work, not implemented repairs.\n\n## Findings\n\n- **Correctness — inclusive endpoint omitted:** `src/count.ts:3` returns\n  `end - start`, despite its documented inclusive integer-count contract.\n  The closed interval 1..3 contains three integers, but the function returns 2.\n  A singleton such as 5..5 similarly returns 0 instead of 1.\n- **Test contract defect:** `src/count.test.ts:4-5` expects 0 for the\n  singleton 5..5, so the passing test preserves the off-by-one defect.\n  There is no test for the requested closed interval 1..3.\n- **Audit/gate blocker:** `scripts/frontend-audit.ts:1-2` prints a fixed\n  report of **21 high-severity advisories**, including `@tiptap/core`, then\n  unconditionally exits 1. It performs no dependency inspection. The count\n  and severity are observed script output, not independently verified\n  vulnerabilities. `package.json` has no dependency inventory, and no lockfile\n  is present to establish affected versions or a remediation scope.\n- **Validation visibility:** `package.json:8` uses `&&`, so an audit failure\n  prevents the test stage of the canonical gate from running.\n  `verify:fast` runs only tests and cannot establish audit success.\n- **API maintainability target:** `inclusiveRangeLength` accepts unrestricted\n  numbers but documents integers. Reversed bounds, fractional values,\n  non-finite inputs, and unsafe integer arithmetic have no defined policy.\n  These are contract decisions to resolve, not additional proven defects.\n- **Greeting review:** `greet` and its Ada test agree on the visible greeting\n  contract; no correctness defect was found in that inspected path.\n\n## Phased improvement roadmap\n\n1. **Phase 1 — Restore the inclusive-range contract.**\n\nFinding: inclusiveRangeLength is incorrect for 1..3.\nActual: 2; Expected: 3\n\n   Update `src/count.ts` to count both endpoints for ordered integer bounds,\n   and correct the singleton expectation in `src/count.test.